window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["computer-architecture"]=[{"id": "CO-U2-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a hardwired control unit, the control signals are generated by", "options": ["a control store that holds microinstructions", "combinational logic fed by the instruction register, the status flags and the timing signals", "an interrupt vector table indexed by the interrupt number", "a microprogram counter together with a mapping ROM"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A control unit is hardwired when its output signals are produced by a fixed combinational network. The inputs to that network are the instruction opcode, the condition flags and the timing (T-state) signals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The instruction register supplies the opcode bits that identify which instruction is executing.</li><li>The status flags supply the conditions on which conditional operations depend.</li><li>A sequence counter and decoder supply the timing state, so the same opcode yields different signals in T0, T1 and so on.</li><li>The network ANDs and ORs these inputs into the individual control lines, so no memory is consulted while the instruction executes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: combinational logic driven by opcode, flags and timing signals (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options A and D describe a microprogrammed unit, where the control word is read from a control store. The two designs differ precisely in whether a memory lookup sits on the control path.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which property of a hardwired control unit makes it preferable to a microprogrammed one?", "options": ["Its control logic can be changed by rewriting the contents of a control store", "It needs no clock signal", "It supports long, complex instruction sets with less hardware", "Its control signals come from fast combinational logic, so the control path is short"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The trade-off between the two control designs is speed against flexibility. Combinational logic answers in gate delays; a microprogrammed unit must first read a microinstruction from the control store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A hardwired unit evaluates a fixed Boolean function of opcode, flags and timing state, so its delay is a few gate levels.</li><li>A microprogrammed unit adds the control-store access time to every step, which is what makes it slower per control word.</li><li>The price of that speed is rigidity: any change to the instruction set means redesigning the logic.</li><li>Hence speed, not modifiability or hardware economy, is the hardwired advantage.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a short, fast combinational control path (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A is the microprogrammed advantage, not the hardwired one; option C is also false, because long complex instruction sets are exactly where microprogramming saves hardware. Every control unit still needs a clock.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A hardwired control unit must generate 6 distinct timing states, one per clock cycle. What is the minimum number of bits in its sequence counter?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sequence counter with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> bits drives a decoder with <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> outputs, so it can distinguish at most <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> timing states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two bits give 4 states, which is fewer than the 6 required.</li><li>Three bits give 8 states, and 8 ≥ 6.</li><li>So the counter needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> 6⌉ = 3</code> bits, leaving two decoder outputs unused.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 6, the number of states, instead of the number of bits needed to encode them. A counter stores a code, not the count of states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An instruction set has 12 instructions and its longest instruction needs 9 timing states. The control unit decodes the opcode with an instruction decoder and steps through the states with a sequence counter. What is the minimum width of the opcode field and of the sequence counter?", "options": ["4 bits opcode and 4 bits sequence counter", "3 bits opcode and 3 bits sequence counter", "4 bits opcode and 3 bits sequence counter", "12 bits opcode and 9 bits sequence counter"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both fields hold a binary code, so each needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉</code> bits for <code class=\"mm\">n</code> distinct values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Opcode: 3 bits address only 8 instructions, so 12 instructions need 4 bits (16 codes, 4 unused).</li><li>Timing states: 3 bits address only 8 states, so 9 states need 4 bits (16 states, 7 unused).</li><li>Both fields are therefore 4 bits wide.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4-bit opcode and 4-bit sequence counter (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding 9 states down to 3 bits because 8 feels close enough, or sizing the opcode by the number of instructions rather than by the number of codes needed to name them.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about hardwired control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["It is faster than microprogrammed control because the control signals come from combinational logic rather than from a control store.", "It is easy to extend when the instruction set changes, because a new instruction only needs new microcode.", "It uses a sequence counter and a decoder to generate the timing states of the instruction cycle.", "A single combinational network can generate every control signal, so no control store is needed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Hardwired control is a fixed Boolean network: fast, rigid, and driven by opcode, flags and timing state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) No memory access sits on the control path, so the control signals settle in gate delays. True.</li><li>(B) Adding an instruction means redesigning the logic and rewiring the network; new microcode is the microprogrammed route. False.</li><li>(C) The sequence counter produces T0, T1, ... and its decoder output is ANDed into the signal equations. True.</li><li>(D) That absence of a control store is exactly what defines the design. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing hardwired control is also the flexible option. Speed and modifiability pull in opposite directions here, and microprogramming is the answer to the second.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences 7 timing states, T0 to T6, from a 3-bit sequence counter feeding a 3-to-8 decoder. How many decoder outputs are never used?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">k</code>-bit counter decodes into <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> outputs; if the machine uses fewer states than that, the surplus outputs stay idle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A 3-bit counter produces 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 codes, so the decoder has 8 outputs.</li><li>States T0 to T6 use 7 of them.</li><li>8 − 7 = 1 output, the code 111, is never asserted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 unused output.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the states as the decoder size, or assuming the spare code is harmless. An unused state must still be handled, since a glitch on the counter can land the machine there.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An instruction needs 20 distinct timing states, and the design decodes every state directly from a binary sequence counter. What is the minimum counter width, and why is a narrower counter unsafe here?", "options": ["4 bits, because states can be reused under different flag conditions", "5 bits, because 4 bits decode to only 16 states while 20 distinct states are required", "20 bits, one bit per state", "4 bits plus a 2-to-1 multiplexer on the decoder output"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A counter of width <code class=\"mm\">k</code> can name <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> distinct states. If every state must be decoded uniquely from the counter alone, the width is forced by the state count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Four bits decode to 16 codes, and 16 &lt; 20, so two states would share a code and the control signals of the two would be asserted together.</li><li>Five bits decode to 32 codes, and 32 ≥ 20, so every state gets its own code and 12 codes remain spare.</li><li>Hence the minimum width is 5 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 bits (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A describes a real but different technique: reusing a state under different flag conditions requires extra decision logic and a branch, which the question excludes by fixing the decoding to the counter alone. Without that extra logic, 4 bits silently merges states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit, the microinstructions are held in", "options": ["the main memory, alongside the program being executed", "the control store, also called the control memory", "the data cache", "the general-purpose register file"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A microprogrammed control unit replaces the control logic with a small memory whose words are control words; each word is a microinstruction that asserts a set of control signals for one step.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The control store is addressed by the microprogram counter.</li><li>Each location holds the control word for one micro-operation.</li><li>The sequencer decides the next address, so the microprogram walks through the steps of the current instruction.</li><li>Main memory and cache hold program and data, never control words.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the control store / control memory (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the control store with main memory. It is a separate, usually read-only array inside the control unit, and its width is the number of control lines rather than the machine word size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A microinstruction format that assigns one bit to each control line, with no encoding of mutually exclusive signals, is called", "options": ["a horizontal microinstruction", "a vertical microinstruction", "a diagonal microinstruction", "a two-level or nano microinstruction"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Horizontal and vertical microcode sit at opposite ends of the width-versus-decoding trade-off. A horizontal word is wide and fully decoded; a vertical word is narrow and encoded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One bit per control line means no decoder is needed and every signal can be asserted independently.</li><li>That independence lets many micro-operations run in parallel within one microinstruction, which is the horizontal advantage.</li><li>The cost is a very wide control word, since the word must carry a bit for every control line.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a horizontal microinstruction (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming wider always means faster in a way that makes vertical code useless. Vertical code trades parallelism for a small control store and is often preferred when the store is a bottleneck.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A control store holds 1024 microinstructions addressed by the microprogram counter. How many bits must the microprogram counter have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The microprogram counter supplies the address of the next microinstruction, so its width must span every location of the control store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1024 = 2<sup>10</sup>.</li><li>Ten bits name 1024 distinct addresses, exactly the number of locations.</li><li>No spare code remains, so every address is a legal microinstruction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1024, the number of locations, instead of the logarithm. Also watch the power-of-two boundary: 1023 locations would still need 10 bits.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A microinstruction consists of 40 control bits and a 10-bit sequencing field. The control store holds 512 microinstructions. What is the total capacity of the control store in bits?", "options": ["25,600 bits", "20,480 bits", "51,200 bits", "22,528 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Control-store capacity is the word width multiplied by the number of words, and the word width includes every field, not only the control bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Word width = 40 + 10 = 50 bits.</li><li>Number of words = 512.</li><li>Capacity = 512 × 50 = 25,600 bits.</li><li>Cross-check: 512 × 40 = 20,480 control bits, and 512 × 10 = 5,120 sequencing bits; the sum is 25,600.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25,600 bits (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B counts only the control field and forgets the sequencing field, which is part of every stored word.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about microprogrammed control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Vertical microprogramming encodes mutually exclusive control signals into fields that a decoder expands, giving a narrow control word at the cost of decoding delay.", "A horizontal microinstruction is wide and can assert many control signals in parallel within a single microinstruction.", "A new instruction can be added by writing new microcode, without redesigning the control logic.", "A microprogrammed control unit always executes an instruction faster than a hardwired unit."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Microprogramming buys flexibility with time: the control word comes from a memory, and encoding it narrowly adds a decoder in series.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Encoded fields shrink the word, and the decoder adds delay. True.</li><li>(B) One bit per signal allows simultaneous assertion, which is the horizontal strength. True.</li><li>(C) The instruction set is defined by the control store contents, so microcode changes are enough. True.</li><li>(D) The control-store access time makes the microprogrammed unit slower per control word, not faster. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating microprogramming as a pure improvement. It is a trade: flexibility and simplicity in exchange for speed and, in the horizontal case, control-store width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A control word has three encoded fields, each of which selects exactly one of 10 mutually exclusive control signals, plus 6 control lines that each require their own bit. What is the minimum width of the control word?", "options": ["18 bits", "24 bits", "30 bits", "36 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An encoded field holding <code class=\"mm\">n</code> mutually exclusive choices needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉</code> bits and a decoder to expand it; a signal that must be controlled independently needs a bit of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each encoded field must name one of 10 signals: ⌈log<sub>2</sub> 10⌉ = 4 bits.</li><li>Three such fields take 3 × 4 = 12 bits.</li><li>The 6 independent lines take 6 bits.</li><li>Total = 12 + 6 = 18 bits.</li><li>Cross-check the encoding capacity: each 4-bit field has 16 codes for 10 signals, so 6 codes per field are spare, which is acceptable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 18 bits (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C comes from adding 10 + 10 + 10 instead of encoding, i.e. from using one bit per signal and forgetting that the field is decoded. Option B comes from dropping the 6 independent lines altogether.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An ALU has 3 function-select inputs. How many distinct operations can it select?", "options": ["3", "6", "9", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Select lines form a binary code, so <code class=\"mm\">k</code> of them name <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> distinct operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Three select bits produce codes 000 through 111.</li><li>That is 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 codes.</li><li>Each code routes one ALU function to the output, so eight operations can be selected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 operations (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3, the number of select lines, or 9 by counting 3 × 3. The select lines are a code, not a count.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In an n-bit ALU, the zero flag is normally produced by", "options": ["ANDing all the result bits", "ORing all the result bits", "NORing all the result bits", "XORing all the result bits"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The zero flag must be 1 exactly when every result bit is 0, which is the definition of a NOR over all bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If any bit is 1, the OR of all bits is 1 and the result is not zero.</li><li>So &ldquo;all bits zero&rdquo; is the complement of the OR, that is the NOR.</li><li>In hardware an n-input NOR is usually built as an OR tree followed by an inverter, which keeps the delay logarithmic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: NOR of all result bits (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> ANDing the bits detects the all-ones case, not zero. A zero-detector must react to the OR being 0, so the flag is the inverse of an OR, not an AND.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a single-bus CPU, a register-to-register ADD traverses: source register output 2 ns, bus 1 ns, ALU 5 ns, bus again 1 ns, and destination register setup 2 ns. What is the minimum clock period in ns?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must cover the longest combinational path between two registers, plus the setup time of the register at the end of that path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Register output to bus: 2 + 1 = 3 ns.</li><li>Through the ALU: 3 + 5 = 8 ns.</li><li>ALU output back onto the bus: 8 + 1 = 9 ns.</li><li>Destination setup: 9 + 2 = 11 ns.</li><li>The bus is used twice because a single bus carries one value at a time, so the two transfers cannot overlap.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding only one bus delay because the bus is one physical wire. It is one wire used twice, so both uses cost time in the same cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A 32-bit ripple-carry adder is built from full adders in which the carry takes 2 ns per stage, and the sum bit of a stage takes a further 3 ns after its carry-in has settled. What is the worst-case delay to the final sum bit S31, measured from the arrival of the operands?", "options": ["62 ns", "64 ns", "65 ns", "96 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a ripple-carry adder the carry propagates serially, and each sum bit needs one more delay after its own carry-in is valid.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Carry into bit <code class=\"mm\">i</code> is valid at <code class=\"mm\">2i</code> ns, since each stage adds 2 ns.</li><li>Bit 31 needs carry <code class=\"mm\">C31</code>, valid at 31 × 2 = 62 ns.</li><li>The sum bit then takes a further 3 ns: 62 + 3 = 65 ns.</li><li>The final carry-out C32 would be valid at 64 ns, which is why 64 is the answer only if the question asks for the carry.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 65 ns (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 64 ns, the carry-out time, instead of the sum time; or multiplying the sum delay by 32, which would wrongly assume the sum bits also ripple.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about datapath timing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The clock period must be at least the longest register-to-register combinational delay plus the destination register setup time.", "Inserting a pipeline register can shorten the clock period, at the cost of more cycles of latency for the same instruction.", "A single-bus datapath executes an ADD in fewer clock cycles than a three-bus datapath.", "A pipeline register placed between the ALU and the destination register shortens the combinational path that fixes the clock period."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period is set by the worst register-to-register path. Pipeline registers cut that path into shorter pieces; buses and their sharing decide how many transfers a step needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) That inequality is the definition of a valid clock period. True.</li><li>(B) Each register halves the logic between two registers, so the period falls while the instruction needs extra cycles to traverse the added stage. True.</li><li>(C) A single bus must be reused for each operand and for the result, so it needs more cycles, not fewer. False.</li><li>(D) Placing the register at the ALU output ends the ALU-to-register path there, so only the shorter segments bound the period. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing pipelining reduces the total work of an instruction. It reduces the clock period and raises throughput, but a single instruction takes more cycles than before.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A datapath register has a clk-to-Q delay of 3 ns, the ALU takes 6 ns, the result multiplexer takes 2 ns, and the destination register needs 2 ns of setup time. What is the minimum clock period?", "options": ["11 ns", "13 ns", "15 ns", "8 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A register-to-register path is bounded by four delays in series: clk-to-Q of the launching register, the combinational logic, any multiplexer on the result path, and the setup time of the capturing register.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Launch: 3 ns.</li><li>ALU: 3 + 6 = 9 ns.</li><li>Result multiplexer: 9 + 2 = 11 ns.</li><li>Setup: 11 + 2 = 13 ns.</li><li>13 ns is therefore the shortest safe period, giving a clock of about 76.9 MHz.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13 ns (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A drops the multiplexer, and option D drops both the clk-to-Q and the setup time. Every element on the path between the two register edges must be counted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The width of a system bus is conventionally quoted as the number of", "options": ["address lines", "data lines", "control lines", "interrupt request lines"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bus is described as an 8-bit, 32-bit or 64-bit bus according to how many bits it can carry in one transfer, which is the data-line count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The data lines move the operand itself, so their number sets the transfer granularity.</li><li>The address lines set the addressable range instead: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> address lines reach <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>n</sup></code> locations.</li><li>Control lines carry timing and command signals and are counted separately.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: data lines (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing bus width with address width. A machine may have 32 data lines and 40 address lines; only the first is called the bus width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a synchronous bus, every transfer is timed with respect to", "options": ["a handshake between the master and the slave", "the ready signal of the slowest device on the bus", "a common clock shared by all devices on the bus", "the interrupt acknowledge cycle of the CPU"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Synchronous buses include a clock line and fix every event to a clock edge; asynchronous buses replace the clock with request and acknowledge handshakes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All devices derive their timing from the same clock, so protocol steps take a known number of cycles.</li><li>That makes the bus simple and fast for devices that can meet the timing.</li><li>Its weakness is that the cycle must be long enough for the slowest device, or a ready/wait line must stretch it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a common clock (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options A and B describe the asynchronous bus, where handshaking lets each device take the time it needs. A ready line can appear in a synchronous bus, but only as an exception to the fixed timing.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A synchronous bus has a 32-bit data path and a 100 MHz clock, and completes one transfer per clock cycle. What is its maximum bandwidth in MB/s? (Use 1 MB = 106 bytes.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "400", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bus bandwidth is the bytes moved per transfer multiplied by the number of transfers per second.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>32 bits = 4 bytes per transfer.</li><li>100 MHz gives 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> transfers per second.</li><li>4 × 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 400 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes per second.</li><li>That is 400 MB/s in decimal megabytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 400 MB/s.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 3200 by using 32 bytes instead of 4, or 419 by dividing by 2<sup>20</sup> when the question specifies decimal megabytes. Also watch the cycle count: one transfer per cycle is given here, and a multi-cycle transfer halves or quarters the figure.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A bus has a 64-bit data path, a 66 MHz clock, and requires 2 clock cycles per data transfer. What is its effective bandwidth?", "options": ["264 MB/s", "528 MB/s", "132 MB/s", "4224 MB/s"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is the transfer size divided by the time one transfer occupies, so a multi-cycle transfer must divide the raw clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>64 bits = 8 bytes per transfer.</li><li>Two cycles per transfer means 66 × 10<sup>6</sup> / 2 = 33 × 10<sup>6</sup> transfers per second.</li><li>8 × 33 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 264 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes per second.</li><li>So the effective bandwidth is 264 MB/s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 264 MB/s (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B ignores the two-cycle transfer, and option C halves twice by also treating the 64-bit path as 4 bytes. Divide the clock once for the cycle count, and use the full data width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus organisation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A three-bus datapath can read both operands and write the result within one clock cycle without an intermediate temporary register.", "In a single-bus organisation each transfer occupies the whole bus for a clock cycle, so the micro-operations of an instruction are serialised.", "A wider data bus shortens the transfer time of a single word even when that word already fits in one transfer.", "A bus with several potential masters needs an arbitration mechanism to decide which one drives it."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of buses fixes how many values can move at once; bus width fixes how much one value can be; arbitration fixes who may drive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Two source buses feed the ALU and a third carries the result back, so no operand has to wait for the bus to be freed. True.</li><li>(B) One bus means one value per cycle, so operands and result are moved one after another. True.</li><li>(C) Once the word fits in a single transfer, extra width cannot shorten that transfer; it only helps wider operands or block transfers. False.</li><li>(D) Contention must be resolved, whether by daisy chain, polling or independent requests. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating bus width as a universal speed-up. Beyond the width of the data being moved, extra lines buy nothing on a single-word transfer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus arbitration are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Daisy-chain arbitration grants the bus by physical position on the chain, so a device nearer the arbiter wins a tie.", "Independent-request arbitration gives each master its own request and grant line, and any priority policy can be implemented.", "Polling arbitration uses a counter to select masters in turn and needs no separate grant line for each master.", "Daisy-chain arbitration gives every master the same worst-case access latency."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three standard schemes resolve contention: daisy chain (grant propagates physically), independent requests (one grant per master), and polling (the arbiter asks masters in turn).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The grant signal passes through each device in order, so the nearest device sees it first and captures it. True.</li><li>(B) With a grant line per master the arbiter may use fixed priority, rotating priority or any other rule. True.</li><li>(C) Polling drives a common select code from a counter, so one set of lines serves all masters. True.</li><li>(D) In a daisy chain a far device must wait for every nearer device to pass the grant along, so latency depends on position. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming daisy chaining is fair because the grant is passed down the line. It is a fixed priority scheme, and the last device in the chain can be starved indefinitely.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit is designed from a control step matrix. What do the rows and the columns of that matrix represent?", "options": ["one row per instruction; one column per control signal", "one row per microinstruction; one column per micro-operation", "one row per timing state (T-state); one column per control signal", "one row per control signal; one column per condition flag"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control step matrix is a two-dimensional table of the control function: one row for each timing state and one column for each control signal, with a 1 in cell (T<sub>i</sub>, S<sub>j</sub>) meaning that S<sub>j</sub> is asserted while the unit is in state T<sub>i</sub>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The unit steps through one timing state per clock, so the number of rows equals the number of T-states.</li><li>Each column corresponds to one control signal that the datapath needs, such as <code class=\"mm\">PCout</code> or <code class=\"mm\">MARin</code>.</li><li>A 1 in a cell means the signal is asserted in that state; a 0 means it is not.</li><li>Instructions do not form the rows: they are decoded separately into instruction lines that gate the rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">one row per timing state (T-state); one column per control signal (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping the axes, as in option A, is the usual slip. Microinstructions (option D) belong to microprogrammed control, where the control word is stored rather than computed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences its 11 timing states with a one-hot ring counter that uses one flip-flop per state. How many flip-flops does the counter require?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A one-hot (ring) sequencer carries one flip-flop per state, so its width grows linearly with the number of states; a binary counter grows logarithmically but needs a decoder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One flip-flop is needed for each state, because exactly one flip-flop is active at a time.</li><li>With 11 states that is 11 flip-flops.</li><li>A binary counter would need ⌈log<sub>2</sub> 11⌉ = 4 flip-flops plus a 4-to-16 decoder and 11 output gates.</li><li>The one-hot form trades flip-flops for the absence of a decoder and of glitch-prone decoded lines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">11</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 4 by recalling only the binary counter. The question fixes the one-hot design, where the count of flip-flops equals the count of states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A control unit is implemented as a PLA with 12 inputs, 8 product terms and 10 outputs, where every input and every output has one programmable connection to every product term. How many programmable connections does the PLA contain?", "options": ["80", "96", "176", "216"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A PLA has two programmable planes: the AND plane forms the product terms from the inputs, and the OR plane sums selected product terms into each output.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>AND plane: every one of the 12 inputs is connected (or not) to each of the 8 product-term lines, so 12 × 8 = 96 connections.</li><li>OR plane: every one of the 8 product terms is connected (or not) to each of the 10 outputs, so 8 × 10 = 80 connections.</li><li>Total = 96 + 80 = 176 programmable connections.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">176 (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the AND plane and answering 96, or using the input count twice and answering 12 × 8 + 12 × 10 = 216. The OR plane is fed by product terms, not by inputs.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences its T-states with a counter feeding a decoder. Which of the following statements about that arrangement are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An n-bit counter with an n-to-2n decoder can provide up to 2n timing states.", "If the END signal clears the counter at the end of every instruction, the next instruction restarts at T0.", "A one-hot ring counter produces the timing states directly and needs no decoder.", "A one-hot ring counter needs fewer flip-flops than a binary counter for the same number of states."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sequencer is a counter plus a decoder, or a one-hot shift register that replaces both; either way the number of states is bounded by the hardware that generates them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: n flip-flops carry 2<sup>n</sup> codes and the decoder turns each code into one state line.</li><li>Option B is true: clearing the counter on END is what makes the state sequence repeat for the next instruction.</li><li>Option C is true: in a ring counter exactly one flip-flop is high at a time, and that flip-flop is the state.</li><li>Option D is false: a ring counter uses one flip-flop per state, while a binary counter uses ⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉, so the ring counter uses more.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reversing the flip-flop trade-off: the one-hot form needs more flip-flops, and buys its saving by removing the decoder and its decoded lines.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit decodes a 4-bit opcode into 16 instruction lines, but the instruction set defines only 9 instructions. The control functions are minimised treating the 7 undefined opcodes as don't-care conditions. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The minimised functions may assert a control signal for an undefined opcode, which is harmless only because a correct program can never fetch one.", "Undefined opcodes must be treated as 1 so that the AND plane stays consistent.", "Using don't-cares cannot change the number of product terms in any control function.", "Leaving the 7 decoder outputs unconnected forces every control function to be 0 for those opcodes."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A don't-care condition lets the minimiser choose either value for that input combination, so the resulting expression is free to be 1 there; the choice is only safe when the combination cannot occur.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each undefined opcode is an input combination that the specification does not constrain, so the truth table entry is X.</li><li>Karnaugh-map or Quine–McCluskey minimisation may then set that entry to 1 if doing so merges implicants, which yields a smaller network.</li><li>The minimised function is therefore not guaranteed to be 0 for those opcodes: the hardware has no defined behaviour for them.</li><li>That is acceptable precisely because a correct program never fetches an undefined opcode; a program that does reaches unspecified behaviour.</li><li>Option C is false because don't-cares often, but not always, reduce the product count. Option D is false because an unconnected input does not constrain the function that the minimiser derives.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The minimised functions may assert a control signal for an undefined opcode (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading 'don't care' as 'must be 0'. It means 'either value is acceptable', which is exactly why the minimised hardware may behave arbitrarily on that opcode.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The signal MARin must be asserted in timing state T0 of every instruction, and in T3 only when the instruction is a memory-reference instruction, for which the decoder produces the signal MR. Which expression implements MARin?", "options": ["T0 · MR + T3", "(T0 + T3) · MR", "T0 · T3 + MR", "T0 + T3 · MR"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each clause of a control function is the AND of the state in which the signal is asserted with the instruction conditions under which it is asserted; the clauses are ORed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The T0 clause is unconditional, so it contributes the single term T0.</li><li>The T3 clause holds only for memory-reference instructions, so it contributes T3 · MR.</li><li>OR the two clauses: MARin = T0 + T3 · MR.</li><li>Check: in T0 the expression is 1 whatever MR is; in T3 it is 1 exactly when MR = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\"><code class=\"mm\">T0 + T3 · MR</code> (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing T0 · MR, which drops the unconditional T0 clause, or T0 · T3, which asks for both states at once — impossible for a counter that is in one state at a time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microinstruction format, which field supplies the address of the next microinstruction when control does not simply fall through to the next word?", "options": ["the control field", "the condition-select field", "the opcode field of the machine instruction", "the sequencing (next-address) field"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A microinstruction that can branch needs a sequencing field: a condition-select part names the flag to test, and a next-address part gives the target address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The control field holds the bits that drive the datapath's control lines.</li><li>The condition-select field only chooses which status flag is tested; it carries no address.</li><li>The opcode field belongs to the machine instruction, not to the microinstruction.</li><li>The next-address (sequencing) field is the one loaded into the microprogram counter when the branch is taken.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the sequencing (next-address) field (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing the condition-select field with the address field. Select names the flag; the address field names the destination.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit each microinstruction occupies exactly one clock cycle of 5 ns. A machine instruction is executed as 6 microinstructions, and a program executes 106 such instructions. What is the total control-store time in milliseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "30", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When every microinstruction takes one clock, the time for a machine instruction is the number of microinstructions times the clock period.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One microinstruction = 5 ns.</li><li>One machine instruction = 6 × 5 ns = 30 ns.</li><li>10<sup>6</sup> instructions = 10<sup>6</sup> × 30 ns = 30 × 10<sup>6</sup> ns.</li><li>30 × 10<sup>6</sup> ns = 30 × 10<sup>−3</sup> s = 30 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">30</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 5 ms by using one microinstruction instead of six, or 0.03 ms by treating the answer as seconds and forgetting the factor of 10<sup>3</sup> between ns and ms.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit, what is the function of the mapping (microprogram) ROM placed between the instruction register and the control store?", "options": ["It holds the control signals for each timing state.", "It stores the condition flags used by conditional microinstructions.", "It translates the instruction opcode into the starting address of that instruction's microprogram.", "It sequences the T-states of the hardwired control network."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The opcode is not a control-store address, so a small ROM converts it into the address at which that instruction's microprogram begins.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At the start of every instruction the microprogram counter must be loaded with the first microinstruction of the routine that implements it.</li><li>The opcode is the ROM's address input; the ROM's data output is the corresponding starting address in the control store.</li><li>Control signals are held in the control store itself, and flags come from the status register, so options A and B describe other blocks.</li><li>Option D describes a hardwired sequencer, which does not exist in this design.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">It translates the instruction opcode into the starting address of that instruction's microprogram (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode can address the control store directly. It cannot: the mapping ROM exists precisely to make that translation, which also lets several opcodes share one microprogram.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A microprogrammed control unit addresses its control store with a microprogram counter. Which of the following statements about that addressing and word format are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The mapping ROM lets two machine instructions with different opcodes share one microprogram, by mapping both opcodes to the same start address.", "The microprogram counter is loaded at the start of an instruction straight from the opcode, with no table in between.", "A microcode subroutine call needs a microprogram return-address stack, because one microprogram counter cannot remember where to return.", "The width of a control-store word is the sum of its control field, its condition-select field and its next-address field."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control store is addressed by the microprogram counter, whose value comes from the mapping ROM at the start of an instruction and from the next-address field or the incrementer afterwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: the mapping ROM is a table, so two opcodes may point at the same entry and share the routine.</li><li>Option B is false: the opcode is not a control-store address; the mapping ROM stands between them, which is exactly what option A relies on.</li><li>Option C is true: with a single counter the return point must be saved somewhere, so microcoded calls use a small stack.</li><li>Option D is true: the three fields partition the word, so their widths add.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode can address the control store directly, which would also make the sharing in option A impossible.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Two machines with the same clock period implement the same instruction set. Machine A uses horizontal microcode: 4 microinstructions per machine instruction, one clock each. Machine B uses vertical microcode: 2 microinstructions per machine instruction, but each microinstruction needs 1 clock plus 2 clocks of control decoding that cannot overlap. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["Machine A is faster: it needs 4 clocks against B's 6, so A's control path is 1.5 times faster.", "Machine B is faster because it uses fewer microinstructions.", "Both need 4 clocks per machine instruction.", "Machine B is faster: 2 clocks against A's 4."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Vertical microcode shortens the control word but lengthens the sequence: each microinstruction pays for decoding, so the comparison must be made in clocks per machine instruction, not in microinstruction count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Machine A: 4 microinstructions × 1 clock = 4 clocks per machine instruction.</li><li>Machine B: each microinstruction costs 1 + 2 = 3 clocks, and there are 2 of them, so 2 × 3 = 6 clocks.</li><li>Compare 4 with 6: A is faster, and the ratio is 6 / 4 = 1.5.</li><li>Option B counts microinstructions and ignores the decoding cost, which is the whole point of the trade-off.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Machine A is faster, 4 clocks against 6 — a factor of 1.5 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Comparing the number of microinstructions (4 against 2) and concluding that B wins. Clocks per machine instruction decide speed, and vertical microcode pays extra clocks for decoding.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A conditional microinstruction carries a condition-select field that picks one of the machine flags. Which statement describes the sequencing correctly?", "options": ["The microprogram counter is loaded from the next-address field when the selected flag is true, and incremented when it is false.", "The microprogram counter is always loaded from the next-address field, whether the flag is true or false.", "The flag value is added to the next-address field to form the branch target.", "The flag is written into the control store so that a later microinstruction can test it."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A conditional branch in microcode multiplexes the microprogram counter's input: the next-address field when the tested flag is true, the incrementer when it is false.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The condition-select field feeds a multiplexer that picks one flag.</li><li>That flag drives a second multiplexer on the microprogram counter's input.</li><li>Flag true → the counter loads the next-address field, so control jumps.</li><li>Flag false → the counter takes the incrementer, so control falls through to the following microinstruction.</li><li>Option B removes the branch entirely; option C describes arithmetic that the datapath does not perform; option D confuses the flag with stored control.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The microprogram counter is loaded from the next-address field when the flag is true and incremented when it is false (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing a conditional branch always jumps. The condition chooses between the next-address field and the increment path, so a false condition simply continues in sequence.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The function-select inputs of an ALU inside a CPU datapath are driven by", "options": ["the data bus", "the status register", "the program counter", "the control unit"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The ALU is a slave of the control unit: which operation it performs is decided by control signals, not by the data it is given.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The ALU operation is part of the instruction being executed, so it is decoded by the control unit.</li><li>The control unit places the operation code on the ALU's function-select inputs.</li><li>The data bus carries operands, the status register receives flags, and the program counter holds an address — none of them selects an operation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the control unit (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode travels on the data bus into the ALU. The opcode is decoded into control signals; only the operands travel on the bus.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a single-bus datapath whose ALU has A and B input registers, which sequence executes ADD R1, R2, that is R1 ← R1 + R2?", "options": ["R1 → A; R2 → B; the ALU forms A + B; the result → R1", "R2 → A; R1 → B; the ALU forms A − B; the result → R1", "R1 → A; the ALU takes R2 straight from the bus in the same clock; the result → R1", "R2 → A; R1 → B; the result → R2"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A single bus carries one value per clock, so two operands cannot reach the ALU together; each must be latched into its own input register first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Clock 1: R1 is gated onto the bus and latched into A.</li><li>Clock 2: R2 is gated onto the bus and latched into B.</li><li>The ALU adds A and B, and the sum is gated onto the bus and latched into R1.</li><li>Option C is impossible on a single bus, and options B and D compute the wrong function or write the wrong destination.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">R1 → A; R2 → B; the ALU forms A + B; the result → R1 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Wanting to fetch both operands in one clock. On a single bus that would require the bus to carry two values at once; that is exactly the cost a two-bus or three-bus datapath removes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about datapath structure and timing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The critical path through the datapath sets the minimum clock period.", "Inserting a pipeline register inside the datapath shortens the critical path but adds latency.", "A register file with only one read port can still supply both ALU operands in the same clock cycle.", "In a single-bus datapath, both ALU operands cannot be transferred in the same clock cycle."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must exceed the longest combinational delay between two registers, and bus and port counts bound how many values can move per clock.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: the clock must be slow enough for the slowest register-to-register path to settle, so that path fixes the period.</li><li>Option B is true: splitting the path shortens each stage but the result now needs an extra cycle to cross the added register.</li><li>Option C is false: one read port delivers one value per cycle, so the second operand must wait.</li><li>Option D is true: a single bus carries one value at a time, so the operands are transferred in separate clocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing a read port with a register file's capacity. A 32-register file with one read port can still deliver only one register's contents per cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A 64-bit adder is built from 4-bit carry-lookahead blocks connected by ripple carry between blocks. Each block produces its carry-out 5 ns after its carry-in arrives, and each of its sum bits 2 ns after the block's carry-in arrives. What is the delay in nanoseconds from the arrival of the carry-in of block 1 to the final sum bit, bit 63?", "options": [], "answer_display": "77", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lookahead removes the carry ripple inside a block but the carry still ripples between blocks, so the total delay is the sum of the inter-block carries plus the last block's sum delay.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>64 bits in 4-bit blocks gives 16 blocks.</li><li>The carry-in of block 1 arrives at t = 0; block k's carry-in arrives at (k − 1) × 5 ns.</li><li>Block 16's carry-in therefore arrives at 15 × 5 = 75 ns — fifteen block-to-block carries, not sixteen, because block 1's carry-in is the adder's own.</li><li>Bits 60–63 are block 16's sum bits, which appear 2 ns after that carry-in: 75 + 2 = 77 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">77</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using 16 carries and answering 16 × 5 + 2 = 82, or forgetting the final sum delay and answering 75. The carry-in of the first block is free; only the 15 carries between blocks cost time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about the status flags produced by an n-bit ALU adder is TRUE?", "options": ["The carry flag and the overflow flag are equal for every addition.", "The zero flag can be derived from the carry flag alone.", "The overflow flag for signed addition is the XOR of the carry into and the carry out of the sign-bit position.", "The sign flag is the carry-out of the most significant bit."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Overflow means the signed result left the representable range, which happens exactly when the carry into the sign bit differs from the carry out of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let c<sub>n−1</sub> be the carry into the sign position and c<sub>n</sub> the carry out of it.</li><li>Overflow = c<sub>n−1</sub> ⊕ c<sub>n</sub>: for example 0111 + 0001 gives c<sub>n−1</sub> = 1, c<sub>n</sub> = 0, so the sum 1000 is the wrong sign.</li><li>The carry flag is simply c<sub>n</sub>, the unsigned carry out, so the two flags are different quantities (option A is false).</li><li>The zero flag is the NOR of all result bits, and the sign flag is the most significant bit of the result, not the carry out of it (options B and D are false).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Overflow is the XOR of the carries into and out of the sign bit (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Equating carry with overflow. Unsigned addition overflows when there is a carry out; signed addition overflows when the carries into and out of the sign bit disagree.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A datapath path runs register → ALU → multiplexer → register. A register has a clk-to-Q delay of 2 ns and a setup time of 1 ns; the ALU takes 7 ns and the multiplexer 1.5 ns. Which statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The minimum clock period is 11.5 ns.", "Doubling the ALU delay to 14 ns exactly halves the maximum clock frequency.", "The maximum clock frequency is about 86.96 MHz.", "Inserting a pipeline register between the ALU and the multiplexer makes the minimum period 10 ns."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must cover clk-to-Q of the source register, the combinational logic, and the setup time of the destination register.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Path delay = 2 + 7 + 1.5 + 1 = 11.5 ns, so option A is true.</li><li>Maximum frequency = 1 / 11.5 ns = 86.96 MHz, so option C is true.</li><li>With a register after the ALU, stage 1 = 2 + 7 + 1 = 10 ns and stage 2 = 2 + 1.5 + 1 = 4.5 ns; the period is the larger, 10 ns, so option D is true.</li><li>With a 14 ns ALU the path is 2 + 14 + 1.5 + 1 = 18.5 ns and the frequency is 54.05 MHz, a factor of 0.62 rather than 0.5, so option B is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Forgetting either the clk-to-Q delay or the setup time when adding up the path, or assuming frequency is inversely proportional to one component's delay. Only the whole path sets the period.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Compared with a single-bus datapath, what is the main advantage of a three-bus datapath?", "options": ["It needs fewer registers.", "Both ALU operands can be transferred in one clock, so an ALU instruction needs fewer clock cycles.", "It cannot perform a memory read, which simplifies the control.", "Its buses are wider, which shortens the ALU delay."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bus count bounds how many values can move per clock: with separate source buses both operands reach the ALU together, and a third bus returns the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On a single bus each operand needs its own clock to reach the ALU input registers, so an ADD takes three transfers.</li><li>With three buses the two source registers drive two buses at once and the result leaves on the third, so the operation completes in one clock.</li><li>Register count and memory capability are unaffected, and bus width is a design choice independent of bus count, so options A, C and D are wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Both ALU operands can be transferred in one clock, so fewer clock cycles are needed (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Thinking more buses make each transfer faster. They do not; they allow more transfers to happen concurrently, which cuts the number of cycles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following is NOT carried on the control bus?", "options": ["the memory read and write strobes", "the address of the operand being fetched", "the interrupt request line", "the bus grant line"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A system bus is split by function: addresses travel on the address bus, data on the data bus, and timing and command signals on the control bus.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read and write strobes tell memory what to do, so they are control signals.</li><li>Interrupt requests and bus grants are requests between devices and the processor, so they are control signals too.</li><li>The address of the operand is carried on the address bus, which exists precisely to carry addresses.</li><li>Hence option B is the one that does not belong to the control bus.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the address of the operand being fetched (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Lumping every 'extra' wire into the control bus. Addresses and data have their own dedicated buses; control carries commands, timing and arbitration.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The number of distinct memory locations a processor can address directly is determined by", "options": ["the width of the data bus", "the width of the address bus", "the clock frequency of the bus", "the number of bus masters"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An address bus of n lines can carry 2<sup>n</sup> distinct patterns, so its width fixes the size of the address space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each address line carries one bit of the address.</li><li>n lines therefore carry 2<sup>n</sup> distinct addresses, and that is the directly addressable space.</li><li>Data-bus width fixes how much is moved per transfer, not how many locations exist; clock frequency fixes transfer rate; the number of masters affects arbitration.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the width of the address bus (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing address space with word size. A 32-bit data bus says nothing about how many locations exist; a 32-bit address bus says there are 2<sup>32</sup> of them.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A processor has a 24-bit address bus. What is the maximum number of distinct byte addresses it can generate?", "options": [], "answer_display": "16777216", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each address line doubles the number of distinguishable addresses, so an n-line address bus addresses 2<sup>n</sup> locations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The bus has 24 lines, so 2<sup>24</sup> patterns are possible.</li><li>2<sup>24</sup> = 16 777 216.</li><li>That is 16 MiB of byte-addressable memory.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">16777216</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Computing 24 × 2<sup>10</sup> = 24 576 or 2<sup>24</sup> / 1024 = 16 384. The address space is a power of two, not a multiple of the width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A synchronous bus has a 64-bit data path and a 100 MHz clock. Each non-burst transfer needs one clock for the address phase and one clock for the data phase. What is the effective bandwidth in MB/s, taking 1 MB = 106 bytes?", "options": [], "answer_display": "400", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is the bytes moved per transfer divided by the clocks a whole transfer occupies, times the clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One transfer moves 64 bits = 8 bytes.</li><li>One transfer occupies 1 address clock + 1 data clock = 2 clocks.</li><li>So the bus moves 8 / 2 = 4 bytes per clock.</li><li>At 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> clocks per second: 4 × 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 400 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes/s = 400 MB/s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">400</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 800 MB/s by dividing the data-path width by the clock alone. The address phase is bus time too, and it halves the useful rate here.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An asynchronous bus uses full handshaking: the master raises Request, the slave answers Acknowledge, data is transferred, then Request and Acknowledge are withdrawn. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The transfer rate is fixed by a clock, so a slow slave must insert wait states.", "Full handshaking cannot detect the absence of a slave device.", "The master must withdraw Request before placing data on the bus.", "Each transfer takes as long as the two devices' response delays require, so a slow slave simply stretches the transfer and no wait-state logic is needed."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Handshaking replaces a shared clock with a request/acknowledge dialogue, so the transfer time adapts to the devices taking part.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No common clock exists; the master waits for the slave's Acknowledge before completing the cycle.</li><li>A slow slave delays its Acknowledge, and the cycle stretches by exactly that much.</li><li>There is therefore nothing for wait-state logic to do: the protocol already accommodates any speed.</li><li>Option A describes a synchronous bus. Option B is false because a missing slave never acknowledges, which the master detects as a timeout or an error. Option C reverses the protocol: data is placed before Request is withdrawn.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Each transfer takes as long as the devices' response delays require, so no wait states are needed (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Carrying synchronous thinking into an asynchronous design. Wait states are a synchronous-bus device; an asynchronous bus absorbs the delay in the handshake itself.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A synchronous bus moves one 64-bit word per data phase. Which of the following statements about how the address phase, bursts and wait states affect throughput are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a burst transfer one address phase serves several data phases, so the effective bandwidth approaches the data-path limit.", "In a non-burst transfer every word repeats the address phase, so the useful fraction of bus time is the data clocks divided by the data plus address clocks.", "A wait state adds a clock to a transfer, so it lowers the effective bandwidth without changing the data-path width.", "Because the address phase and the data phase use different groups of wires, they can always overlap in a non-burst transfer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is bytes per transfer divided by the clocks a whole transfer occupies, so any clock spent not carrying data lowers it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: amortising one address phase over many data phases leaves almost every clock carrying data.</li><li>Option B is true: with one data clock and one address clock the useful fraction is 1/2, which is why the non-burst rate is half the data-path limit.</li><li>Option C is true: a wait state is an extra clock in the denominator, so the rate falls while the width is untouched.</li><li>Option D is false: a non-burst transfer uses the address wires and then the data wires, one after the other, because the slave cannot know which word is wanted until the address has been received.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming separate wire groups mean concurrent phases. The dependency, not the wiring, forces the order: the address must arrive before the data can be selected.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A direct-mapped cache holds 4 KB of data and uses 16-byte blocks. Main-memory block number 1500 maps to which cache line?", "options": ["220", "232", "108", "44"], "answer": "Option (A)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A direct-mapped cache has one line per set, so the line number is the memory block number reduced modulo the number of lines. Only the low bits of the block number select a line; the remaining high bits become the tag.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = cache size / block size = 4096 B / 16 B = 256.</li><li>So the line index is <code class=\"mm\">block mod 256</code>, and 256 = 2<sup>8</sup>, so the low 8 bits of the block number select the line.</li><li>1500 = 5 &times; 256 + 220, so 1500 mod 256 = 220.</li><li>Block 1500 therefore occupies line 220, and its tag is 5 (the quotient).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 220 &mdash; line 220.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The usual slip is to take the byte address modulo the number of lines instead of the block number, or to use 128 lines by forgetting that a 4 KB cache with 16-byte blocks holds 256 blocks. Both give a line that is nowhere near 220.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Line = block number mod (cache size / block size); the quotient is the tag.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32-bit byte-addressed machine has a 16 KB four-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. How many bits are required for the tag?", "options": ["19", "21", "20", "12"], "answer": "Option (C)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A physical address splits into three fields: tag, index (set number) and block offset. The offset is <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(block size)</code>, the index is <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(number of sets)</code>, and the tag takes whatever is left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 32 = 5 bits.</li><li>Number of blocks = 16 KB / 32 B = 512; with 4 ways, number of sets = 512 / 4 = 128, so index = log<sub>2</sub> 128 = 7 bits.</li><li>Tag = 32 &minus; 5 &minus; 7 = 20 bits.</li><li>Check the set count from capacity directly: 16 384 / (32 &times; 4) = 128 sets, which agrees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The common error is to divide the capacity by the block size and stop there, treating 512 blocks as 512 sets; that gives a 9-bit index and a tag of 18. Associativity divides the set count, so the index shrinks by <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(ways)</code> and the tag grows by the same two bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> tag = address bits &minus; log<sub>2</sub>(block size) &minus; log<sub>2</sub>(capacity / (block size &times; ways)).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 2-way set-associative cache has 64 sets. A program accesses memory blocks 0, 64, 0, 64, 0, 64, 0, 64 in that order, starting with an empty cache. How many of these eight accesses are hits?", "options": ["2", "6", "4", "8"], "answer": "Option (B)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a set-associative cache the block number selects the set, <code class=\"mm\">set = block mod (number of sets)</code>, and any of the ways in that set may hold the block. Associativity decides how many blocks that share a set can be resident at once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>0 mod 64 = 0 and 64 mod 64 = 0, so both blocks map to set 0.</li><li>A 2-way set holds two blocks, so blocks 0 and 64 can be resident together.</li><li>Access 1 (block 0) misses and fills way 1; access 2 (block 64) misses and fills way 2.</li><li>Accesses 3 to 8 find the requested block already in the set, so each of the six is a hit.</li><li>Hits = 6, misses = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 hits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A frequent answer is 2 or 0 because the reader assumes the two blocks evict each other. That is exactly what a direct-mapped cache does here &mdash; both blocks map to line 0 and every access misses, giving 0 hits &mdash; but the second way removes the conflict, which is the point of associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two blocks that share a set conflict only when the number of blocks competing exceeds the number of ways.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache has 128 lines. How many tag comparisons must the cache perform in parallel to decide whether a block is present?", "options": ["1", "7", "32", "128"], "answer": "Option (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A fully associative cache has a single set, so there is no index field at all: the address is only a tag plus a block offset. Presence of a block is therefore decided by comparing the requested tag against the tag of every line simultaneously.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of sets = 1, so the index needs log<sub>2</sub> 1 = 0 bits.</li><li>Every one of the 128 lines can hold the block, so all 128 lines must be searched.</li><li>The search is done in parallel by 128 tag comparators, and a hit is the OR of their outputs.</li><li>That parallel comparator bank is why fully associative caches are built small &mdash; the cost grows linearly with the number of lines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 128 comparators.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 7 is the trap: it is log<sub>2</sub> 128, the number of index bits a set-associative cache would need, not the number of comparisons. In a fully associative cache there is no index, so nothing selects one line before the comparison.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fully associative means 0 index bits and one comparator per line.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache is changed from direct-mapped to 4-way set-associative with the same total capacity and the same block size. Which one of the following is a direct consequence?", "options": ["Conflict misses can fall, while the number of capacity misses is unchanged.", "Capacity misses fall, because four blocks can now share one set.", "Compulsory misses fall, because the first access to a block can be served from a sibling way.", "Hit time falls, because fewer tag comparisons are needed per access."], "answer": "Option (A)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Misses are classified by cause. Compulsory misses are the first reference to a block and depend only on the reference stream. Capacity misses are the blocks a cache of that size cannot hold however it is organised. Conflict misses are blocks evicted only because too many of them map to the same set. Associativity attacks the third class alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Total capacity and block size are fixed, so the number of blocks the cache can hold is unchanged and capacity misses cannot change.</li><li>Each block is still seen for the first time once, so compulsory misses cannot change either.</li><li>With 4 ways, up to four blocks that share a set can be resident, so blocks that a direct-mapped cache would have evicted on a conflict now survive: conflict misses can fall.</li><li>Comparing four tags in parallel is slower than comparing one, so hit time rises slightly &mdash; it does not fall.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; conflict misses can fall, capacity misses are unchanged.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the standard error: associativity does not create space, it only re-allocates the freedom to place a block. Option (D) inverts the real cost &mdash; associativity buys miss rate at the price of hit time and comparator hardware.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Associativity trades hit time for conflict misses; it does not touch capacity or compulsory misses.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32 KB four-way set-associative cache uses 64-byte blocks. The byte address 0x00002A40 is presented to it. Which set does the address map to?", "options": ["169", "64", "41", "19"], "answer": "Option (C)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set is selected by the block number, not by the byte address. Convert the address to a block number by dropping the offset bits, then reduce modulo the number of sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits, so the block number is the address shifted right by 6.</li><li>0x00002A40 = 10 816 in decimal; 10 816 / 64 = 169, so the block number is 169.</li><li>Number of sets = 32 768 / (64 &times; 4) = 128.</li><li>Set = 169 mod 128 = 41.</li><li>Equivalently: the index field is bits 6 to 12 of the address, and 10 816 = 0b10_1010_0100_0000 gives index bits 1010101 = 85? &mdash; no: bits 6..12 are 0b0101001 = 41, which agrees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 41 &mdash; set 41.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 169 is the block number, which is the answer only if you forget to reduce modulo the set count. 64 is the byte address reduced modulo 128, which mixes the offset into the index. Associativity is what makes the set count 128 rather than 512; using 512 sets would leave the block number unchanged at 169.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> set = (address / block size) mod (capacity / (block size &times; ways)).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 4% and a miss penalty of 50 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["2.0 cycles", "3.0 cycles", "3.5 cycles", "5.0 cycles"], "answer": "Option (B)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Average memory access time is the hit time plus the expected extra cost of a miss: <code class=\"mm\">AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss rate = 0.04, miss penalty = 50 cycles.</li><li>Average miss cost = 0.04 &times; 50 = 2 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2 = 3 cycles.</li><li>Read as a weighted average: 96% of accesses cost 1 cycle and 4% cost 1 + 50 = 51 cycles, giving 0.96 &times; 1 + 0.04 &times; 51 = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.0 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2.0 cycles is the average miss cost alone; the hit time must be added. 5.0 cycles comes from multiplying the hit time by the miss rate instead of using the hit rate, which double-counts the miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty; the weighted-average form gives the same number.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a local miss rate of 5%. Its misses go to an L2 cache with a hit time of 10 cycles and a local miss rate of 20%; L2 misses go to main memory, which costs 100 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["1.5 cycles", "2.0 cycles", "2.3 cycles", "2.5 cycles"], "answer": "Option (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a two-level hierarchy the L1 miss penalty is itself an average access time for the lower level. Local miss rates multiply down the hierarchy; the cost of an L1 miss is <code class=\"mm\">L2 hit time + (L2 local miss rate &times; memory cost)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of an L1 miss = L2 hit time + L2 local miss rate &times; memory cost = 10 + 0.20 &times; 100 = 10 + 20 = 30 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = L1 hit time + L1 local miss rate &times; (cost of an L1 miss) = 1 + 0.05 &times; 30.</li><li>0.05 &times; 30 = 1.5, so AMAT = 2.5 cycles.</li><li>The global miss rate to memory is 0.05 &times; 0.20 = 1%, so 1% of accesses cost 1 + 30 + 100 = 131 cycles; the weighted average 0.99 &times; 1 + 0.01 &times; 131 = 2.3 &mdash; which is not AMAT, because an access that hits in L2 also pays the 30 cycles. Using the local form as above is the correct bookkeeping.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.5 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2.3 cycles is the trap: it applies the global 1% rate to the full three-level path and forgets that the 5% of accesses that miss L1 but hit L2 also pay for the L2 access. Never mix a global miss rate with a local penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT<sub>L1</sub> = t<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>1</sub> &times; (t<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> &times; t<sub>mem</sub>), with local miss rates.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A processor has a base CPI of 2.0 for a perfect cache. Each instruction makes 1.5 memory accesses on average, the cache miss rate is 3% and the miss penalty is 80 cycles. What is the CPI including memory stalls?", "options": ["5.6", "3.6", "2.6", "4.4"], "answer": "Option (A)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Memory stalls are counted per instruction, not per access: <code class=\"mm\">stall cycles per instruction = memory accesses per instruction &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty</code>, and this is added to the base CPI.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Misses per instruction = 1.5 &times; 0.03 = 0.045.</li><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 0.045 &times; 80 = 3.6.</li><li>CPI = base CPI + stall CPI = 2.0 + 3.6 = 5.6.</li><li>Sanity check: about 4.5 instructions in every 100 each lose 80 cycles, which is 3.6 cycles per instruction on average.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 3.6 is the stall component alone and omits the base CPI; 2.6 comes from forgetting that each instruction makes 1.5 accesses and using 0.03 &times; 80 = 2.4 with a slip. The number of memory accesses per instruction is the factor most often dropped.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CPI = CPI<sub>base</sub> + (accesses per instruction) &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 4-way set-associative L1 has a hit time of 2 cycles and a local miss rate of 3%. Its L2 is unified, with a hit time of 12 cycles and a local miss rate of 25%; main memory costs 120 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["3.02 cycles", "3.14 cycles", "3.26 cycles", "4.26 cycles"], "answer": "Option (C)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nest the levels: the L1 miss penalty is the average time to service a miss at L2, which is the L2 hit time plus the L2 local miss rate times the memory cost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of an L1 miss = 12 + 0.25 &times; 120 = 12 + 30 = 42 cycles.</li><li>L1 miss contribution = 0.03 &times; 42 = 1.26 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 2 + 1.26 = 3.26 cycles.</li><li>Cross-check with the global miss rate: 0.03 &times; 0.25 = 0.75% of accesses go to memory and cost 2 + 42 + 120 = 164 cycles, and 2.25% miss L1 but hit L2 and cost 44 cycles: 0.97 &times; 2 + 0.0225 &times; 44 + 0.0075 &times; 164 = 1.94 + 0.99 + 1.23 = 4.16 &mdash; this over-counts because a memory access already includes the L2 time; the nested form is the correct one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.26 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 3.02 comes from dropping the associativity penalty and starting from 1 cycle. 4.26 comes from applying the 3% rate to memory cost alone (0.03 &times; 120 = 3.6) and ignoring that most L1 misses are served by L2. The L2 local miss rate of 25% applies only to the 3% of accesses that reach L2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = t<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>1</sub>(t<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> t<sub>mem</sub>).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache with 3 lines is initially empty and uses LRU replacement. The reference string is 1, 2, 3, 1, 4. How many hits occur?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer": "Option (B)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LRU evicts the block whose most recent use is furthest in the past, so every hit refreshes a block's recency and pushes it to the most-recently-used end.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 &rarr; miss, cache {1}; LRU order 1.</li><li>2 &rarr; miss, cache {1, 2}; order 1, 2.</li><li>3 &rarr; miss, cache {1, 2, 3}; order 1, 2, 3.</li><li>1 &rarr; hit; order becomes 2, 3, 1.</li><li>4 &rarr; miss, victim is 2 (least recently used); cache {3, 1, 4}.</li><li>Hits = 1, misses = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 hit.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 0 hits comes from evicting 1 on the fourth access, which is what FIFO would do (it evicts the oldest arrival, 1). LRU keeps 1 because it was just used &mdash; the whole difference between the two policies is whether recency or arrival order decides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> On a hit, LRU moves the block to the MRU end; FIFO ignores hits entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses LRU replacement. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, how many hits occur?", "options": ["5", "4", "3", "2"], "answer": "Option (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Run LRU frame by frame, keeping the frames ordered from least to most recently used and evicting from the left on a miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 M: {1}.</li><li>2 M: {1,2}. 3 M: {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: evict 1, {2,3,4}. 1 M: evict 2, {3,4,1}. 2 M: evict 3, {4,1,2}.</li><li>5 M: evict 4, {1,2,5}.</li><li>1 H: {2,5,1}. 2 H: {5,1,2}.</li><li>3 M: evict 5, {1,2,3}. 4 M: evict 1, {2,3,4}. 5 M: evict 2, {3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 10, hits = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 hits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 5 is the optimal (MIN) hit count for this string and 4 is what LRU achieves with four frames; neither is the 3-frame LRU result. Reading the string left to right and refreshing recency on every hit is what produces 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For this string the hit counts are LRU 2, FIFO 3 and optimal 5 with three frames.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, a cache using FIFO replacement produces 9 misses with 3 frames. How many misses does it produce with 4 frames?", "options": ["10", "9", "8", "11"], "answer": "Option (A)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIFO evicts the block that has been in the cache longest, regardless of use. It is not a stack algorithm, so a larger cache does not guarantee a subset relation between the two contents, and the miss count can rise &mdash; this is Belady's anomaly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With 4 frames: 1 M {1}, 2 M {1,2}, 3 M {1,2,3}, 4 M {1,2,3,4}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>5 M, evict 1: {2,3,4,5}. 1 M, evict 2: {3,4,5,1}. 2 M, evict 3: {4,5,1,2}.</li><li>3 M, evict 4: {5,1,2,3}. 4 M, evict 5: {1,2,3,4}. 5 M, evict 1: {2,3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 10, hits = 2 &mdash; one more miss than with three frames.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 misses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 or 9 assumes the miss count must fall as the cache grows. That monotonicity holds for stack algorithms such as LRU and optimal, not for FIFO. The anomaly is real and this string is the standard witness.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Belady's anomaly: FIFO with more frames can miss more; LRU and MIN cannot.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following statements about write policies is correct?", "options": ["A write-back cache needs no dirty bit, because every store is sent to main memory immediately.", "A write-through cache cannot use write-allocate, because the block is already on its way to memory.", "A write-back cache sets a dirty bit on a store, so a block is written to memory only if it has been modified.", "A write-back cache sends every store to main memory and leaves the cached copy unchanged."], "answer": "Option (C)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The write policy decides when main memory is updated. Write-through updates memory on every store and keeps the cache clean; write-back updates memory only when a modified block is evicted, which requires one extra state bit per line, the dirty bit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) describes write-through, not write-back, and its reason is backwards: write-back exists precisely so that memory is not written on every store.</li><li>Write-through and write-allocate are independent choices; a write-through write-allocate cache is a common design.</li><li>Write-back marks the line dirty on the first store to it and writes the block back on eviction, so an unmodified block never costs a memory write.</li><li>Option (D) again describes write-through while keeping the cache stale, which is simply a wrong cache.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The frequent confusion is to think the dirty bit is an optimisation that can be dropped. Without it the cache cannot tell a clean block from a modified one and must write every evicted block back, which turns write-back into the worst of both policies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dirty bit = one bit per line that licenses write-back on eviction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache uses write-back with write-allocate. A loop executes 800 one-word stores to addresses that all lie in the same 64-byte block. The block is not in the cache when the loop starts, and it is evicted exactly once, after the loop ends. How many main-memory write operations does the cache perform?", "options": ["800", "1", "0", "100"], "answer": "Option (B)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-allocate fetches the block on the first write miss; write-back then absorbs all later stores in the cache and writes the block to memory once, on eviction, provided it is dirty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>First store: miss. Write-allocate brings the block in (one memory read) and applies the store, marking the line dirty.</li><li>The remaining 799 stores hit the line in the cache; each updates the cached copy and leaves the dirty bit set.</li><li>On eviction the dirty line is written back once.</li><li>Main-memory writes = 1. (Memory reads = 1, so total block transfers = 2.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 write.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 800 is the write-through answer, and it is the number to compare against: the same loop under write-through with no write-allocate would issue 800 memory writes. 0 ignores the write-back at eviction, which would leave memory stale forever.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-back converts N stores to one block into 1 write-back, at the price of a dirty bit and a possible stale memory copy.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache mapping are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A fully associative cache has no index field, so a lookup compares the tag against every line.", "In a direct-mapped cache each memory block can occupy exactly one line.", "For a fixed capacity and block size, a 4-way set-associative cache has four times as many sets as a direct-mapped cache.", "Two blocks that map to different lines in a direct-mapped cache must also map to different sets in a 2-way cache of the same total size."], "answer": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Mapping is decided by how the address bits are split. Direct-mapped: no choice of line. Fully associative: no index, so the whole cache is one set. Set-associative: the index picks a set and the ways give the choice within it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with one set there is nothing to index, so every line's tag is compared in parallel.</li><li>(B) is true: the index is a function of the block number, so a block has one and only one home line.</li><li>(C) is false: associativity divides the block count into sets. With 512 blocks, direct-mapped has 512 sets and 4-way has 128 sets &mdash; a quarter, not four times.</li><li>(D) is false: a 2-way cache of the same size has half as many sets, so blocks that differed by the direct-mapped line count can now collide. Blocks i and i + 256 in a 512-line direct-mapped cache are in different lines but share a set in a 2-way cache with 256 sets.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the direction error &mdash; more ways means fewer sets for a fixed capacity. Option (D) is the associativity trade-off in disguise: halving the set count is exactly what creates the conflict that the second way then resolves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 5% and a miss penalty of 40 cycles, so its AMAT is 3 cycles. Which of the following changes, each applied on its own, would reduce the AMAT below 3 cycles? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Halving the miss rate to 2.5%.", "Halving the miss penalty to 20 cycles.", "Halving the hit time to 0.5 cycle, while the miss rate rises to 6%.", "Raising the miss penalty to 50 cycles."], "answer": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. Any change must be evaluated in that formula, because a change in one factor can be cancelled by the way it moves another.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Baseline: 1 + 0.05 &times; 40 = 3 cycles.</li><li>(A) 1 + 0.025 &times; 40 = 1 + 1 = 2 cycles &mdash; reduced.</li><li>(B) 1 + 0.05 &times; 20 = 1 + 1 = 2 cycles &mdash; reduced.</li><li>(C) 0.5 + 0.06 &times; 40 = 0.5 + 2.4 = 2.9 cycles &mdash; reduced, but only marginally.</li><li>(D) 1 + 0.05 &times; 50 = 3.5 cycles &mdash; increased, so it does not qualify.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the one usually missed: halving the hit time while the miss rate worsens still helps here (2.9 against 3), but the margin is thin and it would reverse if the miss rate rose past 6.25%. Option (D) is included so that 'any change in the penalty helps' is not assumed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A change helps only if the new AMAT, computed in full, is smaller.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache replacement are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["LRU is a stack algorithm and therefore never exhibits Belady's anomaly.", "FIFO can exhibit Belady's anomaly: a larger cache may produce more misses on the same reference string.", "Optimal (MIN) replacement can be implemented with a fixed, small amount of per-line state.", "For a fixed reference string, LRU with n+1 frames never has more misses than LRU with n frames."], "answer": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A replacement policy is a stack algorithm when the contents of a cache of size n are always a subset of the contents of a cache of size n+1 for the same reference string. Stack algorithms have monotone miss counts; non-stack algorithms need not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the LRU stack property makes the LRU contents at n frames a subset of those at n+1 frames, so misses can only fall as frames are added.</li><li>(B) is true: FIFO is not a stack algorithm. The standard witness string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 gives 9 misses with 3 frames and 10 with 4.</li><li>(C) is false: optimal replacement needs the future reference string, which the hardware cannot know; it is a bound used for evaluation, not an implementation.</li><li>(D) is true and is exactly the stack property stated as a monotonicity claim.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the trap: optimal replacement is easy to compute offline and impossible online. Confusing 'computable in a simulation' with 'implementable in hardware' is the standard slip, and it is why real caches approximate LRU with clock or pseudo-LRU rather than trying to be optimal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stack algorithms (LRU, MIN) are monotone in cache size; FIFO is not.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about write policies are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["With write-allocate, a store that misses first fetches the block into the cache and then applies the store.", "A write-through cache with no write-allocate issues one main-memory write for every store that misses.", "A block that is resident and clean in a write-back cache may hold data newer than main memory.", "Write-back generally reduces main-memory traffic when a workload rewrites the same block many times."], "answer": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent decisions make up a write policy. The update policy says when memory is written (write-through or write-back); the allocation policy says whether a write miss brings the block in (write-allocate or no-write-allocate). Write-back also needs a dirty bit to know which clean blocks may be discarded silently.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: write-allocate brings the block in on a write miss, which is what makes the next store to the same block a hit.</li><li>(B) is true: with no write-allocate the store is not cached, so it is passed straight to memory &mdash; one write per missing store.</li><li>(C) is false: a clean block is by definition identical to memory, so it cannot be newer. Only a dirty block can differ from memory, and that is precisely what the dirty bit records.</li><li>(D) is true: repeated stores to one block collapse into a single write-back at eviction, whereas write-through issues one write per store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) reverses the meaning of clean: 'clean' means the cached copy equals the memory copy, so memory is never stale for a clean block. The same sentence with 'dirty' in place of 'clean' would be true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-through/back is about when memory is written; write-allocate/no-write-allocate is about whether a write miss fetches the block.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about block size and locality are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A larger block exploits spatial locality, because neighbouring words are fetched along with the requested word.", "Increasing the block size always reduces the miss rate.", "Temporal locality means that a block used recently is likely to be used again soon.", "For a fixed memory bandwidth, a larger block increases the miss penalty, because more bytes must be transferred."], "answer": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Block size trades three quantities against each other: the number of blocks a cache of fixed capacity can hold, the fraction of a fetched block that is actually used, and the time to move a block from memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: fetching 64 bytes when 4 are requested is a bet on spatial locality, and it pays off when the neighbours are used.</li><li>(B) is false: as blocks grow, the number of blocks a fixed-capacity cache can hold falls, and blocks fetched for one word may be evicted before their neighbours are used. The miss rate falls and then rises, so there is an optimum block size.</li><li>(C) is true: that is the definition of temporal locality, and it is what makes a cache of any size useful.</li><li>(D) is true: the miss penalty is latency plus transfer time, and transfer time grows with the number of bytes moved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the trap: 'more is better' is false because block size steals capacity. The usual optimum on real machines is a few tens of bytes, and past it the miss rate climbs again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Miss rate against block size is U-shaped, not monotone.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 256 KB cache uses 64-byte blocks and is 8-way set-associative. How many sets does the cache have?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer": "512", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set count is the block count divided by the associativity: <code class=\"mm\">sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of blocks = 256 KB / 64 B = 262 144 / 64 = 4096.</li><li>Number of sets = 4096 / 8 = 512.</li><li>Equivalently 262 144 / (64 &times; 8) = 262 144 / 512 = 512.</li><li>As a check, the index field is log<sub>2</sub> 512 = 9 bits and the offset is log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits, leaving 32 &minus; 15 = 17 tag bits on a 32-bit machine.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 512 sets.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 4096 is the number of blocks, not sets; 8-way associativity divides it. The other slip is to use 1024 for 1 KB and divide by 4, which comes from a 256 Kb reading of the capacity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> sets = blocks / ways; blocks = capacity / block size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 2.5% and a miss penalty of 80 cycles. What is the average memory access time, in cycles?(Enter a number.)", "options": [], "answer": "3", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. The miss rate must be used as a fraction, so 2.5% is 0.025.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss rate = 0.025, miss penalty = 80 cycles.</li><li>Average miss cost = 0.025 &times; 80 = 2 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2 = 3 cycles.</li><li>Weighted-average check: 97.5% of accesses cost 1 cycle and 2.5% cost 81, giving 0.975 + 2.025 = 3.0.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 2.5% as 2.5 instead of 0.025 gives 201 cycles, and reading it as 0.25 gives 21 cycles. The other common slip is to drop the hit time and answer 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Convert every percentage to a fraction before multiplying.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache with 3 frames starts empty. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, what is the minimum number of misses that any replacement policy can achieve?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer": "7", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum over all policies is achieved by optimal (MIN, Belady) replacement: on a miss, evict the block whose next use is furthest in the future, or one that is never used again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 M {1}; 2 M {1,2}; 3 M {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: 1 is used next at position 5, 2 at 6, 3 at 10, so evict 3 &mdash; {1,2,4}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>5 M: 1 next at 8, 2 at 9, 4 at 11, so evict 4 &mdash; {1,2,5}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>3 M: 1 next at 8, 2 at 9, 5 at 12 &mdash; all future uses are ahead, so evict the furthest, 5 &mdash; {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: 1 and 2 are never used again, so evict 1 &mdash; {2,3,4}.</li><li>5 M: 2 is never used again &mdash; evict 2, {3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 7, hits = 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7 misses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 10 is the LRU count for three frames and 9 the FIFO count; both are achievable by real policies but neither is the minimum. Reporting a policy's result as the minimum is the standard error here &mdash; MIN is an offline bound that no online policy can match in general.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal replacement gives the lower bound on misses; LRU and FIFO sit above it.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A write-back, write-allocate cache serves a program that performs 1000 four-byte stores, all falling in 16 distinct 64-byte blocks that are rewritten many times. None of the 16 blocks is in the cache at the start, and all 16 are evicted at the end. How many block write-backs to main memory occur?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer": "16", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-back writes a block to memory when the block is evicted and only if it is dirty. Every block that received at least one store is dirty, so the number of write-backs equals the number of distinct dirty blocks that are evicted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The 1000 stores touch 16 distinct blocks, so at most 16 blocks become dirty.</li><li>Each block receives at least one store (the stores are spread over all 16), so all 16 are dirty.</li><li>A dirty block is written back once, at eviction, no matter how many times it was modified in the cache.</li><li>Write-backs = 16.</li><li>For contrast, a write-through cache with no write-allocate would issue 1000 memory writes, one per store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 block write-backs.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 1000 is the write-through answer. Answering 1000 / 16 = 63 assumes a write-back per store rather than per block. The whole benefit of write-back is that the write-back count depends on the number of distinct dirty blocks, not on the number of stores.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-backs = number of distinct dirty blocks evicted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32-bit byte-addressed machine has a 16 KB four-way set-associative write-back cache with 64-byte blocks. Each line stores its data, one tag, one valid bit and one dirty bit. How many bits of storage does the whole cache need, including data, tags, valid bits and dirty bits?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer": "136704", "solution": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The storage of a cache is per line: the data array holds block size &times; 8 bits, and the tag array holds the tag width plus one valid bit and, for write-back, one dirty bit. The total is lines &times; bits per line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = 16 384 / 64 = 256.</li><li>Number of sets = 256 / 4 = 64, so the index is log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits.</li><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits.</li><li>Tag width = 32 &minus; 6 &minus; 6 = 20 bits.</li><li>Bits per line = 64 &times; 8 data + 20 tag + 1 valid + 1 dirty = 512 + 22 = 534.</li><li>Total = 256 &times; 534 = 136 704 bits, which is 16.68 KB of storage for 16 KB of data.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 136704 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 131 072 is the data alone (16 KB). Forgetting the dirty bit gives 256 &times; 533 = 136 448; forgetting both bits gives 136 192; computing the tag as 32 &minus; 6 &minus; 8 (using 256 sets as if the cache were direct-mapped) gives 18 tag bits and 256 &times; 532 = 136 192. The associativity is what sets the index at 6 bits, not 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> total bits = lines &times; (block size &times; 8 + tag + valid + dirty).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 64 KB direct-mapped cache uses 32-byte blocks on a 32-bit byte-addressed machine. How many bits of the address form the cache index?", "options": ["10", "12", "11", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The index field selects one line, so its width is the base-2 logarithm of the number of lines. The number of lines is the cache capacity divided by the block size, and the offset field is the logarithm of the block size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048.</li><li>Index bits = log<sub>2</sub>(2048) = <b>11</b>.</li><li>Offset bits = log<sub>2</sub>(32) = 5, so the tag takes the remaining 32 &minus; 11 &minus; 5 = 16 bits.</li><li>Check the split: 16 tag + 11 index + 5 offset = 32 bits, which accounts for the whole address.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 index bits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5 by reading the block size as the index, or answering 12 by dividing the capacity by 32 twice. The index counts lines, not bytes and not blocks of memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the three field widths in the order offset, index, tag, and check that they add up to the address width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 16-way set-associative cache has 512 sets and 32-byte blocks. What is the data capacity of the cache?", "options": ["256 KB", "16 KB", "128 KB", "512 KB"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A set-associative cache stores one block per way, so the data capacity is (number of sets) &times; (ways) &times; (block size). Associativity adds blocks per set without changing the set count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data per set = 16 ways &times; 32 B = 512 B.</li><li>Total data = 512 sets &times; 512 B = 262144 B.</li><li>262144 B / 1024 = <b>256 KB</b>.</li><li>Check the index field: log<sub>2</sub>(512) = 9 index bits and log<sub>2</sub>(32) = 5 offset bits, so the tag is 32 &minus; 14 = 18 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 256 KB</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the associativity gives 512 &times; 32 B = 16 KB, which is the capacity of a direct-mapped cache with the same 512 lines. Every way holds its own block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Capacity = sets &times; ways &times; block size. In a direct-mapped cache the number of ways is 1, which is why the formula collapses to lines &times; block size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 32 KB direct-mapped cache with 16-byte blocks serves a machine with a 32-bit byte address and 4 GB of physical memory. How many distinct main-memory blocks map to any one cache line?", "options": ["65536", "262144", "2048", "131072"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a direct-mapped cache each line is claimed by every memory block whose index bits equal that line's number. The count is therefore (total memory blocks) / (number of lines), and the index bits are the only address bits a line ignores.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Lines in the cache = 32768 / 16 = 2048 = 2<sup>11</sup>.</li><li>Total memory blocks = 2<sup>32</sup> / 16 = 2<sup>28</sup> = 268435456.</li><li>Blocks per line = 2<sup>28</sup> / 2<sup>11</sup> = 2<sup>17</sup> = <b>131072</b>.</li><li>Cross-check with the tag: 17 tag bits, and 2<sup>17</sup> distinct tag values can share one line.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 131072 blocks per line</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 2048, the number of lines, or 65536 by using 2<sup>16</sup>. The tag width, not the line count, gives the number of blocks competing for a line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every line has 2<sup>tag bits</sup> claimants. Compute the tag width once and the answer follows.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 2-way set-associative cache has 256 sets and 16-byte blocks on a 32-bit byte-addressed machine. Which bits of the address form the set index?", "options": ["bits 3 to 10", "bits 4 to 11", "bits 5 to 12", "bits 12 to 19"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The address is cut from the least significant end: the offset occupies the lowest bits, the index the next bits, and the tag the highest. The offset width comes from the block size alone, and the index width from the set count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Offset = log<sub>2</sub>(16) = 4 bits, occupying bits 0 to 3.</li><li>Index = log<sub>2</sub>(256) = 8 bits, occupying the next eight positions, bits 4 to 11.</li><li>Tag = 32 &minus; 4 &minus; 8 = 20 bits, occupying bits 12 to 31.</li><li>The associativity does not enter the split: 2 ways change the tag width, not the index width.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: bits 4 to 11</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Starting the index at bit 0 ignores the offset, and using 3 offset bits (option A) is the rule for 8-byte blocks. Associativity is also often wrongly subtracted from the index width.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix offset first, then index, then give the rest to the tag. Only the block size and the set count matter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 2 cycles, a miss rate of 5% and a miss penalty of 60 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["5 cycles", "4 cycles", "3 cycles", "8 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT is the hit time plus the expected extra cost of a miss: AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. The miss penalty is counted from the moment the miss is detected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss contribution = 0.05 &times; 60 = 3 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 2 + 3 = <b>5 cycles</b>.</li><li>Sanity check: a 5% miss rate can at most add 0.05 &times; 60 = 3 cycles, so 5 is the only option above the 2-cycle hit time by the right amount.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying the hit time by the miss rate, or adding the whole 60 cycles. Only the miss fraction pays the penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. Every performance question in this unit starts from that line.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 2 GHz processor has a base CPI of 1.2, makes 1.2 memory accesses per instruction, has a cache miss rate of 2% and a miss penalty of 100 cycles. How long does a program of 10<sup>9</sup> instructions take?", "options": ["0.9 s", "2.4 s", "1.8 s", "3.6 s"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Memory stalls enter the CPI equation as accesses per instruction &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty, and execution time is total cycles divided by clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 1.2 &times; 0.02 &times; 100 = 2.4.</li><li>CPI including stalls = 1.2 + 2.4 = 3.6.</li><li>Total cycles = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>.</li><li>Time = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> / (2 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Hz) = <b>1.8 s</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.8 s</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using one memory access per instruction gives 1.2 + 0.02 &times; 100 = 3.2 and 1.6 s, and forgetting the base CPI gives 1.2 s. Both slip through if the formula is applied from memory instead of from the definition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Execution time = (base CPI + stalls per instruction) &times; instruction count / clock rate. Keep the accesses-per-instruction factor.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a miss penalty of 100 cycles. What is the largest miss rate for which the average memory access time does not exceed 3 cycles?", "options": ["3%", "2%", "1.5%", "5%"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty solved for the miss rate rather than evaluated with it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Set 1 + m &times; 100 &le; 3.</li><li>m &times; 100 &le; 2, so m &le; 0.02.</li><li>The largest admissible miss rate is therefore <b>2%</b>.</li><li>Check: 1 + 0.02 &times; 100 = 3 cycles exactly, and 3% would give 4 cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2%</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the budget as 3% because the target is 3 cycles. The 3 cycles include the 1-cycle hit time, so only 2 cycles of penalty are available.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Budget questions: subtract the hit time first, then divide by the penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "An L1 cache has a local miss rate of 6%. Every L1 miss is sent to an L2 whose local miss rate is 20%. What percentage of all L1 accesses reach main memory?", "options": ["1.2%", "6%", "20%", "26%"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The global miss rate of a level is the fraction of all references that miss at that level, and in a two-level hierarchy it is the product of the local miss rates along the path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An L1 access reaches L2 only on the 6% of accesses that miss in L1.</li><li>Of those, 20% miss in L2 and go to memory: 0.06 &times; 0.20 = 0.012.</li><li>So <b>1.2%</b> of all L1 accesses reach main memory.</li><li>Equivalently, the global L2 miss rate is 1.2% while the local L2 miss rate stays 20%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.2%</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two rates (26%) or quoting 20%. Local rates multiply; only global rates add up along a path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Local miss rate is per level, global miss rate is per original reference. Convert before comparing levels.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a local miss rate of 4%. Its L2 has a hit time of 8 cycles and a local miss rate of 30%; a main-memory access costs 150 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["3.30 cycles", "2.12 cycles", "3.12 cycles", "7.12 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a hierarchy the levels nest: the cost of an L1 miss is the L2 hit time plus the L2 miss rate times the cost below it. Multiplying the outer rate by that whole inner cost gives the AMAT.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of one L1 miss = 8 + 0.30 &times; 150 = 8 + 45 = 53 cycles.</li><li>L1 miss contribution = 0.04 &times; 53 = 2.12 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2.12 = <b>3.12 cycles</b>.</li><li>Check the alternative route: 0.04 &times; 8 = 0.32 and 0.04 &times; 0.30 &times; 150 = 1.8, and 1 + 0.32 + 1.8 = 3.12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.12 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 150 as the whole L1 miss penalty gives 1 + 0.04 &times; 153 = 7.12 and ignores the L2 hit entirely. Dropping the L1 hit time gives 2.12.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nest the levels from the bottom up: inner cost first, then multiply by the outer miss rate, then add the outer hit time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A fully associative cache with 4 frames starts empty and uses LRU replacement. The reference string is 2, 4, 6, 2, 4, 8, 2, 4, 6, 8. How many hits occur?", "options": ["4", "5", "3", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under LRU the victim is the least recently used frame, so the frame contents must be kept in order of last use. A fully associative cache has no index, so any frame may hold any block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2, 4, 6 all miss and fill the four frames partially: {2, 4, 6}.</li><li>2 hits, then 4 hits; the frames become {6, 2, 4} in LRU order.</li><li>8 misses and replaces 6, the least recently used: {2, 4, 8}.</li><li>2 hits, 4 hits, 6 hits and 8 hits follow, since nothing is evicted once the four blocks 2, 4, 6, 8 all fit.</li><li>Hits = positions 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 = <b>6</b> hits against 4 misses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 hits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the cache as having three frames gives 4 hits; the fourth frame is what lets 2, 4, 6 and 8 coexist after the last miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the frame contents after every reference. LRU answers are only as good as the bookkeeping.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses FIFO replacement. For the reference string 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5, 8, 9, 7, 9, how many misses occur?", "options": ["12", "14", "10", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIFO evicts the block that has been resident longest, regardless of how often or how recently it was used. A hit does not change the queue order, which is what separates FIFO from LRU.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>3, 1, 4 miss and fill the cache; order of arrival is 3, 1, 4.</li><li>1 hits (no reordering under FIFO). 5 misses and evicts 3: queue 1, 4, 5.</li><li>9 misses, evicting 1: queue 4, 5, 9. Then 2 misses (evict 4), 6 misses (evict 5), 5 misses (evict 9), 3 misses (evict 2): queue 6, 5, 3.</li><li>5 hits. 8 misses and evicts 6: queue 5, 3, 8. Then 9 misses (evict 5), 7 misses (evict 3), and 9 hits.</li><li>Misses = 12, hits = 3, which accounts for all 15 references.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reordering on a hit, which turns the trace into LRU and gives a smaller count; or evicting the least recently used block instead of the oldest arrival.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> FIFO keeps an arrival queue and ignores hits. If you find yourself moving a block on a hit, you are tracing LRU.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "For the reference string 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 5, 3, 2, 5, 2 with 3 frames, LRU and optimal (MIN) replacement give different miss counts. Which pair is correct, written as (LRU, optimal)?", "options": ["(6, 7)", "(7, 6)", "(7, 7)", "(6, 6)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LRU looks backwards at the last use; optimal looks forwards at the next use. Because optimal has strictly more information, its miss count can never exceed LRU's on the same string and frame count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>LRU trace: 2 M, 3 M, 2 H, 1 M, 5 M, 2 H, 4 M, 5 H, 3 M, 2 M, 5 H, 2 H.</li><li>That gives <b>7</b> misses and 5 hits for LRU.</li><li>Optimal trace: on the miss for 4 the victim is 1 (never used again), and on the miss for 3 the victim is 4: misses fall to <b>6</b>.</li><li>So the pair is (7, 6), and optimal is better by exactly one replacement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (7, 6)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the order to (6, 7) by assuming LRU is the better policy, or answering (6, 6) by tracing optimal with LRU's victim choice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal is a bound, not an algorithm. Any online policy's miss count is at least the optimal one for the same string.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache with no write-allocate serves a loop of 400 stores, each to a different word that is not in the cache. How many main-memory write operations does the loop cause?", "options": ["0", "100", "400", "800"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-through sends every store to memory immediately. No-write-allocate means a store that misses does not fetch the block, so a miss costs a write but no read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the 400 stores misses, because none of the words is resident.</li><li>No-write-allocate suppresses the fetch, so no read is issued.</li><li>Write-through issues one memory write per store, so there are <b>400</b> writes.</li><li>Total memory transactions = 400 writes + 0 reads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 400 memory writes</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 0 by confusing no-write-allocate with no-write-through, or 800 by charging a fill read for each miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two independent choices: allocate or not (does a store miss fetch?) and through or back (does a store reach memory now?). Here it is no-allocate plus through.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache with write-allocate serves 2000 stores of 4 bytes, all to 8 distinct 64-byte blocks that are not resident when the loop begins. Counting reads and writes together, how many main-memory accesses does the loop cause?", "options": ["2000", "8", "16", "2008"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-allocate turns the first store to a block into a read-modify-write: the block is fetched, and then every store in it writes through. The fetch happens once per block, not once per store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Eight blocks are fetched, so there are 8 memory reads.</li><li>All 2000 stores write through, so there are 2000 memory writes.</li><li>Total accesses = 8 + 2000 = <b>2008</b>.</li><li>For contrast, write-back with write-allocate would issue 8 reads and 8 write-backs, that is 16 accesses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2008 memory accesses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2000 forgets the eight fills, and 16 is the write-back answer to the same workload. The policy, not the workload, decides which number applies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count fills once per block and writes once per store for write-through, or once per block for write-back.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache has no write buffer, so every store waits 20 cycles for main memory. Stores are 12% of all instructions and every other instruction takes exactly 1 cycle. What is the effective CPI?", "options": ["1.12", "3.4", "2.4", "4.0"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Without a write buffer the processor stalls for the full memory write on every store, so the store cost enters the CPI as (fraction of stores) &times; (write time).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 0.12 &times; 20 = 2.4.</li><li>Base CPI for the remaining instructions = 1.0.</li><li>Effective CPI = 1.0 + 2.4 = <b>3.4</b>.</li><li>With a four-entry write buffer the processor would continue until the buffer filled, so the stall would fall to roughly one store in twenty rather than every store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1.12 by adding 12% to the CPI as a fraction rather than as cycles, or 2.4 by dropping the base CPI. A store stalls the whole pipeline for 20 cycles here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stalls per instruction = frequency &times; cost per occurrence. That one line covers caches, stores, branches and interrupts.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache address mapping are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["For a fixed capacity and block size, doubling the associativity halves the number of sets and adds one bit to the tag.", "The offset field width depends only on the block size.", "A fully associative cache has no index field, so every line's tag is compared in parallel.", "Two addresses that differ only in their tag bits always map to different sets."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The address split is determined by three independent quantities: the block size fixes the offset, the number of sets fixes the index, and whatever remains is the tag. Associativity and capacity together fix the number of sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways), so doubling ways halves the sets, removing one index bit and adding it to the tag.</li><li>(B) True. The offset addresses a byte inside a block, so it is log<sub>2</sub>(block size) whatever the associativity or the capacity.</li><li>(C) True. With one set there is nothing to index; the tag must be compared against every line, which is why a fully associative lookup costs one comparator per line.</li><li>(D) False. The index bits decide the set. Two addresses that differ only in the tag bits agree on the index and therefore land in the <i>same</i> set, where they compete for the ways.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the trap: tag bits identify a block within a set, not a set, so differing tags mean the same set, which is exactly how conflicts arise.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Index selects the set, tag identifies the block inside it, offset selects the byte. Map every claim about mapping onto those three roles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache performance are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["The global miss rate of a two-level hierarchy is the product of the two local miss rates.", "Memory stall cycles per instruction equal memory accesses per instruction times miss rate times miss penalty.", "Increasing the cache capacity always lowers the average memory access time.", "If the hit time rises by one cycle while the miss rate halves with the miss penalty unchanged at 20 cycles, the AMAT must fall."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT is a sum of two terms, the hit time and the expected miss cost, and improvements to one term can be cancelled by damage to the other. That is the whole content of the capacity and associativity trade-offs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Only the fraction that misses at L1 reaches L2, so the fraction reaching memory is the product of the local rates.</li><li>(B) True. That product is the definition of stall cycles per instruction, and it is the standard bridge from miss rate to CPI.</li><li>(C) False. A larger cache usually has a longer hit time because of the wider tag check and the longer access path, and that raises the first term for every access, hit or miss.</li><li>(D) False. Original AMAT = 1 + 20m and new AMAT = 2 + 10m. The new value is smaller only when m &gt; 0.1; for a 5% miss rate it is larger (2.5 against 2.0).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) feels true because the miss rate halved, but a one-cycle hit-time penalty is paid by every access while the miss saving is paid only by the misses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compare AMAT terms, not miss rates. A change that touches the hit time affects 100% of accesses.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache replacement are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["LRU is a stack algorithm and therefore never exhibits Belady's anomaly.", "Optimal replacement needs knowledge of future references, so it cannot be implemented online.", "FIFO is a stack algorithm.", "For a fixed reference string and frame count, LRU can never have fewer misses than optimal replacement."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack algorithm has the property that the set of blocks held with n frames is always a subset of the set held with n+1 frames, which forbids Belady's anomaly. LRU has that property because its ordering does not depend on the frame count; FIFO's does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. LRU's victim choice depends only on the past reference order, so adding a frame can only add blocks to the resident set.</li><li>(B) True. Optimal picks the block whose next use is furthest away, which requires the future of the reference stream; it is a bound used for comparison.</li><li>(C) False. FIFO's resident set depends on the frame count, so a larger cache can hold a worse set; the classic 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 string misses 9 times with 3 frames and 10 times with 4.</li><li>(D) True. Optimal minimises misses over all policies, so no other policy can beat it on the same string and frame count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the standard error: FIFO looks like a stack algorithm because it is simple, but the anomaly on the classic string proves it is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only stack algorithms are immune to Belady's anomaly. LRU and optimal are stack algorithms; FIFO and random are not.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about write policies are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A write-back cache may hold data that is newer than the corresponding main-memory contents.", "A write-through cache needs no dirty bit, because main memory is always up to date.", "With no write-allocate, a store that misses does not fetch the block into the cache.", "A write buffer removes every store stall, however long the loop of stores runs."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The dirty bit exists only to answer one question at replacement time: is the cached copy the newest one? Write-through never leaves a stale memory word, so the question never arises.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. A store to a resident line sets the dirty bit and memory keeps the old value until the line is evicted.</li><li>(B) True. Every store has already reached memory, so eviction needs no write-back and no dirty state.</li><li>(C) True. No-write-allocate writes around the cache, which is the usual partner of write-through because the block would not be reused soon.</li><li>(D) False. A buffer absorbs bursts, but if stores arrive faster than memory drains it, it fills and the processor stalls again; its benefit depends on the drain rate.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the classic overstatement. A write buffer smooths bursts; it cannot exceed the memory bandwidth over a long run.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask what each mechanism is for: dirty bit for eviction, allocate for reuse, buffer for burst absorption. None of them removes a bandwidth limit.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about write-back caches are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Replacing a dirty block costs a write-back in addition to the fill that caused the replacement.", "In a write-back cache a read miss that evicts a dirty block needs two memory transactions.", "Write-back reduces memory traffic when a block is rewritten many times before it is evicted.", "For a read-only workload, write-through generates more main-memory write traffic than write-back."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A write-back cache defers memory writes until eviction, so its traffic is counted in dirty blocks rather than in stores. The price is paid on the replacement path, where a dirty victim must be written before the new block is filled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The victim's data must be saved before its frame is reused, so the replacement carries an extra write.</li><li>(B) True. The dirty victim is written out and the requested block is read in, which is why write-back caches often need a write buffer on the eviction path.</li><li>(C) True. Many stores to one block produce a single write-back, whereas write-through would produce one memory write per store.</li><li>(D) False. A read-only workload never dirties a line, so write-through issues no writes either; both policies generate zero write traffic and the same read traffic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) sounds like a general law but fails on the degenerate case: with no writes, there is nothing for either policy to write.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-back trades write traffic for a more expensive miss path. Say which quantity is being compared before claiming one policy is better.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 512 KB 8-way set-associative cache uses 64-byte blocks. How many sets does the cache contain?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1024", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways). Associativity divides the line count into sets rather than adding capacity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Capacity in bytes = 512 &times; 1024 = 524288.</li><li>Block size &times; ways = 64 &times; 8 = 512 bytes per set.</li><li>Sets = 524288 / 512 = <b>1024</b>.</li><li>Check the index width: log<sub>2</sub>(1024) = 10 bits, and the offset is log<sub>2</sub>(64) = 6 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1024 sets</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing only by the block size gives 8192, the number of lines, and that would be the set count of a direct-mapped cache of the same size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways). Lines = sets &times; ways.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 4 cycles, a miss rate of 3% and a miss penalty of 120 cycles. What is the average memory access time in cycles?(Enter a number; decimals are allowed.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7.6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty, with the penalty measured from miss detection to the arrival of the block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss contribution = 0.03 &times; 120 = 3.6 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 4 + 3.6 = <b>7.6 cycles</b>.</li><li>Sanity check: 7.6 lies between the 4-cycle hit time and the 124-cycle all-miss time, as it must.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.6 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding the 3.6 to 4 or dropping the hit time gives 3.6, which is below the hit time itself and therefore impossible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The answer must lie between the hit time and the hit time plus the penalty. Use that interval as a free check.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses LRU replacement and starts empty. For the reference string 4, 1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, how many misses occur?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every first reference to a block misses, and every reference to a block that has been evicted misses again. Tracking the last-use order is what makes LRU decidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>4, 1, 2 miss and fill the cache; order of last use is 4, 1, 2.</li><li>4 hits and becomes most recent: order 1, 2, 4.</li><li>5 misses, evicting 1: order 2, 4, 5. Then 3 misses, evicting 2: order 4, 5, 3.</li><li>4 hits: order 5, 3, 4. Then 1 misses, evicting 5: order 3, 4, 1.</li><li>2 misses, evicting 3: order 4, 1, 2. Finally 3 misses, evicting 4.</li><li>Misses = 8 (three cold misses plus five replacement misses) and hits = 3, accounting for all 11 references.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 9 by evicting the most recently used block at one step, or 7 by forgetting that the final 3 was evicted earlier.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three cold misses are unavoidable with three distinct blocks; every further miss is a replacement you must justify.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through L1 forwards every store to an L2 that is write-back with 64-byte blocks. A program performs 5000 four-byte stores into a 4 KB region; all 64 blocks of the region stay resident in L2 for the whole loop and are evicted only after it ends. How many 64-byte blocks does L2 write to main memory in total?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-back traffic is counted in dirty blocks that leave the cache, not in stores that arrive. An L2 that stays resident accumulates dirty bits and writes a block only when that block is evicted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The region is 4 KB, so it spans 4096 / 64 = 64 blocks.</li><li>All 64 blocks stay resident for the whole loop, so nothing is written back during it: the write-back count inside the loop is 0.</li><li>Every block receives stores, so every block is dirty when the loop ends.</li><li>The final evictions write all <b>64</b> blocks to memory, that is 64 &times; 64 = 4096 bytes.</li><li>For contrast, a write-through L2 would have issued 5000 &times; 4 = 20000 bytes of writes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5000 by counting stores instead of blocks, or 16 by assuming a 16-byte write-back granularity. A dirty block is written back whole, once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a write-back cache, count distinct dirty blocks. For a write-through cache, count stores. The two are different denominators.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 3-frame cache starts empty. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 6, what is the minimum number of misses that any replacement policy can achieve?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum is the optimal (MIN) count: on every miss, evict the resident block whose next use is furthest in the future, or one that is never used again. No policy can do better, because a miss is unavoidable whenever the requested block is absent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1, 2, 3 are cold misses: 3 misses, frames {1, 2, 3}.</li><li>4 misses. Next uses are 1 at position 6, 2 at position 5 and 3 at position 12, so 3 is furthest away and is evicted: 4 misses, frames {1, 2, 4}.</li><li>2 and 1 hit. 5 misses and evicts 4, which is never used again: 5 misses, frames {1, 2, 5}.</li><li>6 misses and evicts 5, also never used again: 6 misses, frames {1, 2, 6}.</li><li>2, 1 and 2 hit. 3 misses and evicts 1, whose next use is at position 17 against 13 for 6 and 16 for 2: 7 misses, frames {2, 6, 3}.</li><li>7 misses and evicts 2, whose next use is at position 16 against 14 for 6 and 15 for 3: 8 misses, frames {6, 3, 7}.</li><li>6 and 3 hit. 2 misses and evicts 7, never used again: 9 misses, frames {6, 3, 2}.</li><li>1 misses and evicts 6, whose next use is at position 20 against 18 for 2 and 19 for 3: 10 misses, frames {3, 2, 1}.</li><li>2 and 3 hit. The final 6 misses, and any frame may go: 11 misses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the LRU count of 15, which is a property of LRU rather than a lower bound, or forgetting that the last reference to 6 is a miss because 6 was evicted at position 14.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal is a bound. Any implementable policy, including LRU and FIFO, can only do worse on the same string.</p>"}];